	abstract = {Examines uses that the brain may make of the massive flow of sensory information that occurs without any associated rewards or punishments in the light of connectionist models of unsupervised learning and older ideas (e.g., the cognitive maps and working models of E. C. Tolman [1932] and K. J. Craik [1943]). Also examined are the idea that redundancy is important for understanding perception, the physiology of sensory pathways, and pattern recognition. The redundancy of sensory messages may provide the knowledge incorporated in the maps or models, and some of this knowledge can be obtained by observations of mean, variance, and covariance of sensory messages, and perhaps by minimum entropy coding. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)},
